# Decorator

Add behavior by wrapping an object that keeps the **same interface**. Stack wrappers in any order.

This is **not** the same as TypeScript/Nest `@Injectable()`, `@UseGuards()`, method decorators. Those are language/framework metadata. GoF Decorator is object wrapping.

## Anatomy

- Component interface (`OrderProcessor.process`).
- Concrete component (core work).
- Decorators that take `wrapped: OrderProcessor` and call through.

```ts
export class ValidationDecorator implements OrderProcessor {
  constructor(private readonly wrapped: OrderProcessor) {}

  process(order: OrderData): ProcessedOrder {
    if (order.items.length === 0) {
      throw AppError.badRequest('Order must contain at least one item');
    }
    const result = this.wrapped.process(order);
    result.steps.push('validated');
    return result;
  }
}

let processor: OrderProcessor = new BaseOrderProcessor();
processor = new ValidationDecorator(processor);
processor = new LoggingDecorator(processor);
return processor.process(order);
```

## Nest mapping

Prefer framework tools when they already wrap the request:

| Need | Prefer |
| --- | --- |
| Validate input | pipes (`ZodValidationPipe`) |
| Auth / session | guards / pipes (`ShareSessionPipe`) |
| Map errors | exception filters |
| Cross-cutting log/timing | interceptors or a TS method decorator |

Use GoF Decorator when composing **domain** processors at runtime (optional discount + logging + validation on an order engine), not for HTTP.

`GraphqlExceptionFilter` extending `BaseExceptionFilter` is inheritance + Adapter, not Decorator.

## When to use

- Independent behaviors combined per request.
- Subclassing every combination would explode.

## When not to use

- One fixed pipeline — a workflow or Chain of Responsibility is clearer.
- Unstable component interface (every wrapper churns).

Related: `nest-composite.mdc`, `nest-proxy.mdc`, `nest-chain-of-responsibility.mdc`.
